Lanes holding a MIX of every entry kind: plain suffix, `domain:` exact (the
`--domain` flag), `geosite:`, and a CIDR — in all three lanes.

Exists for two claims the other scenarios cannot make:

1. A `domain:` line must not leak into the suffix rules. Both list readers pick
   suffixes by "isn't a CIDR", a test `domain:z.com` passes, so the exclusion is
   load-bearing and invisible if you only read the code.
2. `exact.example` is present BOTH ways, and `example.com` is exact here while a
   plain `example.com` also exists — so the render's ordering (every `domain`
   rule ahead of every `domain_suffix`) and the explainer's "exact wins at any
   length" agree, and the single-lane invariant treats the two kinds as the
   different entries they are.
